# ─── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
API_PORT=4000
API_HOST=0.0.0.0

# ─── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────
# One Postgres while it's a monolith. After you extract a module,
# give that service its OWN DATABASE_URL and drop its tables here.
# Runs in Docker via docker-compose.yml (`docker compose up -d postgres`) —
# on :5435 because :5432/:5433/:5434 are already used by other projects on
# this machine.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://era:era@localhost:5435/era?schema=public

# ─── CORS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fixed allowlist of browser origins allowed to call the API with credentials
# (the session cookie). In development the API ALSO accepts any private-LAN
# origin (10.x/172.16-31.x/192.168.x) on ports 5173/5174 — see platform/cors.ts
# — so accessing the SPAs from another device on your network just works
# without editing this. This list is what still applies in production.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:5174

# ─── AI Assistant (ChatPage.tsx) ───────────────────────
# Optional — the API starts fine without it, but apps/client's chat assistant (Tier 1, real
# LLM) returns a clear error until it's set. Get a key at console.anthropic.com → Settings →
# API Keys. Billed separately from any claude.ai subscription.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# Signs the chat's "lead created in this conversation" reference, so a visitor can correct their
# own contact details without being able to edit anyone else's lead. Any long random string, e.g.
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Optional — without it, references stop working after an API restart.
# CHAT_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ─── Telegram bot (same AI assistant, on Telegram) ─────
# Optional. In Telegram, message @BotFather → /newbot → paste the token it gives you here.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Unset (local dev): the API long-polls Telegram — works on localhost, no public address needed.
# Set to this API's public HTTPS address in production → the bot switches to a webhook at
# <PUBLIC_API_URL>/webhooks/telegram (registered automatically on startup).
# PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com
# The customer website — bot replies link to property pages there.
# PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ─── Inbox (staff taking over bot chats) ───────────────
# A chat your team took over goes back to the AI when the customer has waited this many minutes
# with no staff reply (the AI answers them; the chat stays flagged). 0 = never.
# INBOX_AUTO_HANDBACK_MINUTES=30
# A taken-over chat with no activity for this many hours quietly returns to the AI. 0 = never.
# INBOX_IDLE_RELEASE_HOURS=12

# ─── Event bus ─────────────────────────────────────────
# inprocess = modular monolith (default).
# nats      = after the first module is extracted (wire NatsEventBus first).
EVENT_BUS=inprocess
NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222

# ─── Frontends ─────────────────────────────────────────
# apps/client (customer website, :5173) and apps/admin (back office, :5174)
# are separate Vite apps, each with `server.host: true` (LAN-reachable; Vite
# prints the Network URL to use from another device).
#
# Leave VITE_CLIENT_URL / VITE_API_BASE_URL UNSET for normal dev — the code
# falls back to `http://${window.location.hostname}:<port>`, which resolves
# correctly whether you open the app via localhost or a LAN IP. Only set them
# to pin a fixed cross-origin value (e.g. a real domain in production).
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000
# VITE_CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:5173
